const e={id:"boss-4",title:"The Master Storyteller's Trial",subtitle:"Prove your mastery of the complete Science of Storytelling",passingScore:80,sections:[{type:"intro",title:"The Final Trial",content:`You've completed all 35 chapters of **The Science of Storytelling**. This is the ultimate test — not just Part 4, but a synthesis of everything you've learned.

This assessment covers:
- Plot as character externalized
- The sacred flaw and its anatomy
- Crisis, climax, and transformation
- Endings and psychological satisfaction
- The meaning of story
- The storyteller's mindset and practice

**Passing score: 80%**

Prove you've become a Master Storyteller!`},{type:"quiz",questions:[{id:"boss4-q1",type:"multiple-choice",question:"What is the relationship between plot and character?",options:["Plot and character are independent","Plot is character externalized","Character follows plot","Plot is more important than character"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"Plot is character externalized — the same event creates different plots for different characters because plot emerges from the collision between character and circumstance."},{id:"boss4-q2",type:"multiple-choice",question:'What makes a flaw "sacred"?',options:["It is religious in nature","It formed as survival mechanism, is woven into identity, and challenging it feels like annihilation","The character is aware of it","It is morally wrong"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"A sacred flaw formed as a survival mechanism, is woven into identity, challenging it feels like annihilation, and the character is often blind to it."},{id:"boss4-q3",type:"multiple-choice",question:"What are the three components of a sacred flaw?",options:["Beginning, middle, end","Want, need, change","False belief, protective function, blindness","Cause, effect, consequence"],correctIndex:2,explanation:"Every sacred flaw has a false belief (distorted view), a protective function (what need it served), and blindness (why they can't see it)."},{id:"boss4-q4",type:"multiple-choice",question:"How does flaw escalation work through a story?",options:["The flaw stays constant","Denial → Doubling Down → Desperation → Crisis","The flaw immediately resolves","Other characters fix the flaw"],correctIndex:1,explanation:`Under plot pressure, the sacred flaw escalates: denial ("that wasn't about my flaw"), doubling down, desperation, and finally crisis where escape is impossible.`},{id:"boss4-q5",type:"multiple-choice",question:"What distinguishes crisis from climax?",options:["They are the same","Crisis is the impossible choice; climax is the resulting action","Climax comes first","Crisis is external; climax is internal"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"Crisis is the moment of impossible choice that forces confrontation with the flaw. Climax is the action that results from and demonstrates that choice."},{id:"boss4-q6",type:"multiple-choice",question:'What makes a choice "impossible" in a crisis?',options:["It physically cannot be made","Both options cost something essential — flaw vs. need","There is no time limit","Multiple characters must agree"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"An impossible choice forces sacrifice either way — the character must choose between their flaw (safety/identity) and their need (growth/truth)."},{id:"boss4-q7",type:"multiple-choice",question:'What is the function of the "dark night of the soul"?',options:["To introduce romantic subplot","To force flaw confrontation without defenses and create maximum uncertainty","To explain backstory","To give the hero rest"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"The dark night strips away defenses, forces confrontation with the flaw at rock bottom, and creates maximum uncertainty about whether transformation will occur."},{id:"boss4-q8",type:"multiple-choice",question:"What are the four psychological functions of endings?",options:["Action, dialogue, description, exposition","Closure, meaning, resonance, reframing","Beginning, middle, climax, resolution","Setup, conflict, twist, conclusion"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"Endings provide closure (answering the dramatic question), meaning (revealing significance), resonance (emotional echo), and reframing (changing how we see everything before)."},{id:"boss4-q9",type:"multiple-choice",question:'What makes an ending feel "earned"?',options:["It is happy","Transformation is prepared, gradual, costly, and consistent","It is unexpected","All questions are answered"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"Earned endings have transformation that was prepared (seeds planted), gradual (not sudden), costly (real sacrifice), and consistent with character."},{id:"boss4-q10",type:"multiple-choice",question:'Why are stories considered "survival technology"?',options:["They help us escape danger","They simulate experience, transmit culture, and create meaning","They are entertaining","They replace real experience"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"Stories evolved as survival technology: simulating dangerous situations safely, transmitting cultural knowledge, binding communities, and creating meaning in chaos."},{id:"boss4-q11",type:"multiple-choice",question:'What is "narrative identity"?',options:["A character's name","The sense of self we construct through personal stories","A story's genre","The author's biography"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"Narrative identity is the psychological concept that we construct our sense of self through the stories we tell about our past, future, and daily experience."},{id:"boss4-q12",type:"multiple-choice",question:"What is the storyteller's moral responsibility?",options:["To always include explicit morals","Honesty, complexity, empathy, and awareness of implicit teachings","To avoid all darkness","To make readers happy"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"Storytellers' responsibility involves showing honest costs, resisting oversimplification, humanizing characters, and being aware of implicit teachings."},{id:"boss4-q13",type:"multiple-choice",question:"What is the recommended starting point for story creation?",options:["The plot outline","The setting details","The character's sacred flaw","The ending twist"],correctIndex:2,explanation:"Start with the character's sacred flaw. Plot, conflict, world, and transformation all emerge from understanding this core element."},{id:"boss4-q14",type:"multiple-choice",question:'What is the "plot-first thinking" trap?',options:["Planning too carefully","Creating events without character logic driving them","Writing too slowly","Having too few scenes"],correctIndex:1,explanation:`Plot-first thinking creates events that don't emerge from character logic. The escape is asking "Why would THIS specific person do this?"`},{id:"boss4-q15",type:"multiple-choice",question:'What is the core principle: "Character IS Plot"?',options:["Characters should be plot devices","Events gain meaning through character response; the same event creates different plots for different characters","Plot doesn't matter","All characters are the same"],correctIndex:1,explanation:"Character IS Plot means events only matter because of how they affect specific characters. The same situation creates entirely different stories for different people."}]},{type:"summary",title:"🌟 Course Complete! 🌟",points:["You've mastered **The Science of Storytelling**!","You understand **brains, flaws, plots, and meaning**","You can craft **psychologically authentic characters**","You know how to design **flaw-testing plots**","You can create **satisfying, earned endings**","You understand the **moral responsibility** of storytellers","Now go **create stories that matter**! 📚✨"]}]};export{e as bossAssessment4};
